    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (81.21) acting as the clear anchor of the offense. Facing a favorable matchup against Bennett (xERA 4.88), **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** benefits from a substantial 19% boost in SP skill valuation and a strong dynamic platoon edge, making him the highest-projected asset in our lineup. Following closely is **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (73.66), who draws a prime spot in the two-hole against Avila. His efficiency is buoyed by a 14% dynamic platoon advantage and favorable order placement, positioning him as a high-upside play for both AVG and volume stats. **Drake Baldwin** (70.07) also commands attention; while his base projection is moderate, the combination of a weak opposing starter in Whisenhunt and an advantageous #2 spot in the order elevates him into our elite tier for catchers today.\n\nDespite the heavy focus on high-efficiency starters, we have to navigate a few tough matchups against elite arms. **Kyle Tucker** (59.54) and **Andy Pages** (57.55) are both forced into a defensive posture against McClanahan, as the opposing starter's suppressed xERA (3.79) creates a significant headwind. We are monitoring **Sam Antonacci** (45.55) closely, as his negative platoon split against the lefty Rod\u00f3n pushes him toward the bottom of our roster; he remains a start only due to our tactical requirement to keep stolen base potential on the field. The toughest bench decision today was **Brayan Rocchio** (51.22). While he sits just above the marginal threshold, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Jackson Merrill** (67.93) and **Gunnar Henderson** (64.32), whose order placement and matchup against Kirby provide more reliable pathways to R and RBI production in a 5x5 format where every efficiency point matters.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the bottom of the order, specifically **Nasim Nunez** (52.85), whose sprint speed is vital for our pursuit of SB, but whose value is compromised by a #9 spot in the order and a slight penalty for the catcher\u2019s ability to control the running game. We are currently navigating a thin bench with **Luis Robert Jr.** remaining sidelined on the IL, leaving us limited flexibility should weather or late lineup scratches materialize. While we are optimized for today, we are banking on the high floors of **Dominic Smith** (60.21) and **Miguel Vargas** (59.73) to stabilize the categories where our primary stars face difficult pitcher-park combinations.",
